  'Liberal Arts / Humanities Distribution',
  'Liberal Arts / Humanities Distribution',
  'This course introduces students to the liberal arts and humanities as disciplined ways of interpreting texts, images, places, performances, beliefs, histories, and cultural practices. It is especially useful for architecture and design-adjacent students because it connects visual/spatial literacy with writing, ethical reasoning, historical context, social analysis, and global cultural awareness.',
  $lahdjson${"slug": "liberal-arts-humanities-distribution", "title": "Liberal Arts / Humanities Distribution", "description": "This course introduces students to the liberal arts and humanities as disciplined ways of interpreting texts, images, places, performances, beliefs, histories, and cultural practices. It is especially useful for architecture and design-adjacent students because it connects visual/spatial literacy with writing, ethical reasoning, historical context, social analysis, and global cultural awareness.", "topics": [{"id": "f206b11f-f077-4a22-ae31-7f75735b4681", "type": "topic", "title": "Liberal Arts and Humanities: Purpose, Methods, and Public Value", "description": "This opening topic explains why humanities learning belongs in professional and design education: it builds judgment, interpretation, ethical awareness, cultural literacy, and the ability to connect specialized work to human contexts.", "children": [{"id": "437fddc3-c73e-4ba0-b1ba-b7f3408afeb5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "What the humanities study", "children": [{"id": "54f131d2-83a9-4a04-af4b-97147062f0f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Human meaning, memory, value, and expression"}, {"id": "6478f29e-5b50-4b2c-9546-3dea69e539ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Interpretation rather than only measurement"}, {"id": "87f74d6e-646d-4271-99fe-88bac8e02036", "type": "concept", "title": "Dialogue between past and present"}], "description": "Students learn the range of humanistic inquiry: texts, images, artifacts, places, performances, beliefs, languages, institutions, and lived experience."}, {"id": "f9509b9c-55b2-4bf5-adb3-a8577054d851", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Liberal education and civic judgment", "children": [{"id": "0a79b438-1a8e-4d7b-8445-336e98582bea", "type": "concept", "title": "Civic literacy and democratic conversation"}, {"id": "c0b03585-48a7-4bf5-b9b8-a405e7f80a8e", "type": "concept", "title": "Intellectual humility and curiosity"}, {"id": "272338b9-ede9-4bcf-8d7b-5a70d3797c2a", "type": "concept", "title": "Professional judgment beyond technical competence"}], "description": "This subtopic shows how broad learning develops flexible thinking, public reasoning, and the ability to deliberate across difference."}, {"id": "ee347fe4-8454-44dc-b362-a90ee54baa88", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interdisciplinary habits of mind", "children": [{"id": "905cd122-dc7f-42c9-b104-2dc25fbf7311", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparing disciplinary questions"}, {"id": "2352df86-4a19-46a7-8fee-4413f60034a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Choosing appropriate evidence"}, {"id": "8b13ac87-80be-42d0-b2c3-958ec2145d71", "type": "concept", "title": "Synthesizing rather than merely sampling"}], "description": "Students practice moving between literature, history, philosophy, art, religion, and social science without flattening their distinct methods."}]}, {"id": "494df0c9-0d8a-4753-becb-d26548aa6cd7", "type": "topic", "title": "Critical Reading, Close Looking, and Interpretive Evidence", "description": "Humanities courses train attention. This topic builds the core skill of slowing down, observing carefully, and turning evidence into persuasive interpretation.", "children": [{"id": "cfd59fc1-eb87-47d3-ab13-9ec93d11b539", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Close reading of texts", "children": [{"id": "fbdb420f-59a9-4a62-9bcc-4d7267653556", "type": "concept", "title": "Diction, imagery, tone, and syntax"}, {"id": "d09f17d2-dc9e-4993-95e5-c37827226c93", "type": "concept", "title": "Narrator, audience, and point of view"}, {"id": "5efb3223-6f48-4519-9341-194959e1b47c", "type": "concept", "title": "Patterns, tensions, and ambiguities"}], "description": "Students learn how wording, structure, voice, genre, and omission shape meaning."}, {"id": "25bc4299-614c-477f-b5a7-beb209ec67ee", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Close looking at images and objects", "children": [{"id": "653ab2d1-a943-4509-8b80-271059712c63", "type": "concept", "title": "Composition, scale, color, material, and technique"}, {"id": "0ad24ce5-6a4d-4d46-86ee-07b0b50ddb46", "type": "concept", "title": "Iconography and symbolism"}, {"id": "69798004-c97c-402d-b6aa-278d35a583cb", "type": "concept", "title": "Site, viewer, and use"}], "description": "Visual analysis helps students describe what they see before jumping to interpretation or judgment."}, {"id": "967a0b6b-cf20-4c76-bd88-9c80723f62de", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evidence, inference, and interpretation", "children": [{"id": "99ceb4fe-8a23-4b75-abec-98b542af8933", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary and secondary evidence"}, {"id": "006c6bf6-2922-4a46-8f8f-9eaaa42c3699", "type": "concept", "title": "Contextual inference"}, {"id": "0edaf6ab-9be8-42a4-9464-6cb09de94076", "type": "concept", "title": "Competing interpretations"}], "description": "Students distinguish evidence from opinion and learn how claims become credible in humanistic work."}]}, {"id": "a7b1eb9e-410e-484f-b035-0e5f43c8f816", "type": "topic", "title": "Writing, Argument, Rhetoric, and Revision", "description": "A distribution course should leave students better able to write clearly, reason publicly, and revise their own thinking. This topic makes writing a central intellectual practice.", "children": [{"id": "615dd5b0-52e7-4107-9e3d-a5df5509c4e9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Claims, stakes, and thesis development", "children": [{"id": "0b1eb6c4-1cc2-4a11-8880-b2831a88890a", "type": "concept", "title": "Analytical questions"}, {"id": "0ec2d4a6-b756-402c-8af3-60101c382d99", "type": "concept", "title": "Thesis scope and contestability"}, {"id": "d25c2fe4-5bf1-45cf-8093-c3d158d87fdd", "type": "concept", "title": "Stakes for readers and fields"}], "description": "Students learn to make arguments that matter, not just summaries that report information."}, {"id": "f0a90795-9bb7-40e8-b3a2-27f2ddaf774e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Rhetorical awareness", "children": [{"id": "63467ad2-66eb-4281-bf98-34fccb1b11b1", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethos, pathos, logos, and kairos"}, {"id": "94344d3e-4721-4628-b41e-4e753ddeacde", "type": "concept", "title": "Style, clarity, and voice"}, {"id": "de7fcf33-4bbe-436b-81ad-22628bd46357", "type": "concept", "title": "Public, academic, and professional genres"}], "description": "Students examine how audience, purpose, genre, and medium shape communication."}, {"id": "8f3a1163-351d-477e-bb85-70388c3c5872", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Revision as thinking", "children": [{"id": "e566a064-6306-4d72-99d1-6db71aca4f70", "type": "concept", "title": "Reverse outlining"}, {"id": "4a381ff3-1b65-4336-9e06-0d3e93c17710", "type": "concept", "title": "Peer feedback and response"}, {"id": "53ff7221-653a-429a-86a6-5029eb24eaa0", "type": "concept", "title": "Editing for precision and rhythm"}], "description": "Revision is treated as a method for sharpening evidence, structure, and intellectual honesty."}]}, {"id": "c9447b9a-0af0-40ec-b015-020e14407fef", "type": "topic", "title": "Literature, Narrative, Poetry, and Drama", "description": "Literary study expands attention to language, imagination, identity, genre, and the emotional complexity of human experience.", "children": [{"id": "bf4675b2-d8cb-49b6-9939-4431a315bed5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Narrative and fiction", "children": [{"id": "512b38ed-58fa-4995-8a00-7f6679a3c038", "type": "concept", "title": "Plot, setting, and characterization"}, {"id": "65deede9-afe6-4f5a-b05e-0f0de457e48d", "type": "concept", "title": "Unreliable narration and perspective"}, {"id": "34cabbea-163b-461b-ad09-c58a5ecd0038", "type": "concept", "title": "Realism, modernism, speculative fiction, and world literature"}], "description": "Students study how stories organize time, character, causality, memory, and social worlds."}, {"id": "047bfc65-010c-4496-a765-60394f6680d7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Poetry and compressed language", "children": [{"id": "5dff3aec-7c59-44ec-a4c7-9edc468f6419", "type": "concept", "title": "Line, meter, rhythm, and form"}, {"id": "046f8123-b9f8-4e7f-ad73-db3e91f9c51f", "type": "concept", "title": "Image, metaphor, and symbol"}, {"id": "60ff64e4-da8b-4408-b3dc-8fc447815024", "type": "concept", "title": "Voice, lyric, and performance"}], "description": "Poetry teaches precision, pattern, sound, and ambiguity in their most concentrated forms."}, {"id": "95bb5b04-0d64-46e7-861b-5f174256fe13", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Drama and performance", "children": [{"id": "a46898fc-0654-4abb-bda7-90aa81e1bdde", "type": "concept", "title": "Dialogue, staging, and conflict"}, {"id": "b271a6b6-41be-4fd5-ad99-83320293880a", "type": "concept", "title": "Tragedy, comedy, ritual, and modern theatre"}, {"id": "83a2ec49-482a-4b71-ad6a-39ff8fba21f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience, performance, and adaptation"}], "description": "Drama reveals how text becomes embodied action in shared space."}]}, {"id": "f6e6c853-b16e-4cb3-a5e8-63c1f25af431", "type": "topic", "title": "History, Memory, Archives, and Historical Thinking", "description": "Historical thinking helps students understand change over time, evaluate sources, and see how societies remember, forget, and argue about the past.", "children": [{"id": "c151a968-e3da-4b32-b765-250b9eed4e5f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical questions and causation", "children": [{"id": "9f8dfb64-4e03-49e2-ba6e-cf00d41f1812", "type": "concept", "title": "Chronology and periodization"}, {"id": "e8da36f3-117c-46f8-b815-250a71edf4a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Causation and contingency"}, {"id": "76bea439-9def-4a0e-af23-e20c61f6b2c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Scale: local, national, regional, and global"}], "description": "Students learn to ask questions about change, continuity, contingency, and consequence."}, {"id": "8e44bfe4-c9da-4347-987b-d18f4fcba8dd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Primary sources and archives", "children": [{"id": "67db98a9-030a-47c0-beb0-9e8c9f63ccf1", "type": "concept", "title": "Manuscripts, newspapers, photographs, maps, oral histories"}, {"id": "3273673b-1fb4-49c4-b95a-07c438291a06", "type": "concept", "title": "Archival silences and power"}, {"id": "ce616bff-e983-422a-a25c-9ccdb9b343b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Citation and provenance"}], "description": "Students learn how historical evidence is created, preserved, classified, and interpreted."}, {"id": "d18a86f3-23b8-4b20-b193-2604eb57a19b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Memory, monuments, and public history", "children": [{"id": "0aef0a2a-cc4e-4825-9edd-23f32930ec8c", "type": "concept", "title": "Museums and memorials"}, {"id": "180d96a8-2a9c-41dc-8ff0-ea415670fa25", "type": "concept", "title": "Contested monuments"}, {"id": "6caed7ef-2e0c-43d3-86f2-0bcbc9cc1025", "type": "concept", "title": "Community history and public scholarship"}], "description": "This subtopic connects scholarship to public debates about heritage, commemoration, and collective identity."}]}, {"id": "de518dbb-5fd8-4f3c-9a83-7bcab97b9f6a", "type": "topic", "title": "Philosophy, Ethics, Meaning, and Critical Reasoning", "description": "Philosophy gives students tools for clarifying concepts, testing assumptions, and asking what follows if their beliefs are true.", "children": [{"id": "5fe51cc3-30cb-48c8-964d-210dd8293287", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Logic and argument analysis", "children": [{"id": "8cd9fb97-b482-4493-9366-17deb17acac4", "type": "concept", "title": "Deductive and inductive arguments"}, {"id": "aab3ce39-82aa-4bc1-a836-61dae0bf6d8a", "type": "concept", "title": "Fallacies and charitable reconstruction"}, {"id": "f865fbe6-22ff-496a-a639-8136a1266d5b", "type": "concept", "title": "Definitions and conceptual clarity"}], "description": "Students learn to identify valid reasoning, hidden assumptions, and weak inferences."}, {"id": "ec0c8bdc-4798-4712-a3d8-38a8800765b2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethical theories and moral judgment", "children": [{"id": "b370640d-9217-4c2a-b0ce-64a26cd9f899", "type": "concept", "title": "Consequentialism, deontology, virtue ethics, care ethics"}, {"id": "529e3892-565c-42ae-8ce5-a2d6bc580b60", "type": "concept", "title": "Rights, duties, harms, and goods"}, {"id": "e51925c5-7657-4473-b619-6622a6b0999b", "type": "concept", "title": "Professional and civic ethics"}], "description": "Students compare major approaches to moral reasoning and apply them to difficult cases."}, {"id": "7b825a0a-8077-4c0a-836e-397ba6fedee5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Meaning, knowledge, and reality", "children": [{"id": "a20b5351-e333-4443-85db-af26cd9dd8a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Epistemology and skepticism"}, {"id": "7757712d-8064-4dbb-b149-acde0e20f94e", "type": "concept", "title": "Personhood and identity"}, {"id": "4368dedc-1503-48ac-9e97-e4c8111c0fe8", "type": "concept", "title": "Free will, responsibility, and meaning"}], "description": "Classic questions about truth, knowledge, freedom, identity, and value deepen students' ability to think at the root level."}]}, {"id": "23c52b3e-5966-4847-9ac7-0785dffb5d37", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion, Myth, Ritual, and Symbolic Worlds", "description": "This topic treats religion as a major human form of meaning-making, social organization, artistic expression, ethical imagination, and conflict.", "children": [{"id": "a3e30b57-fe61-43e0-85a9-fbff050a8d1a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scripture, story, and interpretation", "children": [{"id": "6b430c29-63c0-44fb-87e5-15bbc95cc4c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon, commentary, and reception"}, {"id": "e26612c0-4041-4924-97a2-cc6bf6ab9ece", "type": "concept", "title": "Creation, exile, liberation, wisdom, and apocalypse"}, {"id": "4f428c84-403f-4fda-8ed9-7d29a84aa017", "type": "concept", "title": "Hermeneutics and translation"}], "description": "Students examine sacred texts and myths as layered works shaped by translation, tradition, and community."}, {"id": "a7519637-ae50-4178-8b62-b6bf0ce029eb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ritual, practice, and community", "children": [{"id": "a7b0cdd0-b732-4023-911a-2dd373db2060", "type": "concept", "title": "Pilgrimage, prayer, festival, and sacrifice"}, {"id": "98902f86-85e1-4dfe-ba05-5eb5c5afc003", "type": "concept", "title": "Embodiment and material religion"}, {"id": "e77d0191-b3d6-4cbb-9a2d-61d9e55a69e6", "type": "concept", "title": "Authority, institutions, and belonging"}], "description": "Religions are studied as lived systems of practice, not only as sets of doctrines."}, {"id": "3b6eb3b5-fefa-47ba-b618-10023973410e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religion, pluralism, and modernity", "children": [{"id": "ba964c71-bb85-4cd5-b48c-994f43c262c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Religion and politics"}, {"id": "f03347b0-2b90-48c2-9101-42eed1b4edbc", "type": "concept", "title": "Pluralism and interfaith ethics"}, {"id": "105cb670-3aaf-43c6-a48c-f9d481e7043c", "type": "concept", "title": "Modern critique and renewal"}], "description": "Students explore secularization, interreligious encounter, fundamentalism, and public life."}]}, {"id": "c9ae0110-842b-4601-a9d8-42835cbdfd07", "type": "topic", "title": "Visual Arts, Architecture, Design, and Material Culture", "description": "Because this course is referenced in architecture, visual and spatial literacy are essential. Students learn how objects, buildings, and images carry cultural arguments.", "children": [{"id": "84b95a28-d56c-4725-be46-d26cde5f3a91", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Art-historical description and context", "children": [{"id": "10d8e07e-5617-4d40-8099-56be602dc9eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Medium, technique, and style"}, {"id": "83ec54b2-796c-4b7e-b1df-56bb63189624", "type": "concept", "title": "Patronage, institution, and audience"}, {"id": "f8d6dab3-bc68-49f4-bf2d-9a37ae980f28", "type": "concept", "title": "Period, region, and movement"}], "description": "Students learn to connect formal description with historical setting and social use."}, {"id": "a40b5864-bdda-4825-859c-32fbb4a39588", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Architecture as cultural text", "children": [{"id": "6a7759b5-445f-4944-8594-4be7d4d9a675", "type": "concept", "title": "Form, function, ornament, and structure"}, {"id": "f6562bee-db7e-4207-b77d-66d157befbda", "type": "concept", "title": "Urban context and public life"}, {"id": "cb27dc4c-537b-4393-a431-a301ef00d2da", "type": "concept", "title": "Power, memory, and identity in built form"}], "description": "Buildings are read as spatial, technical, social, and symbolic artifacts."}, {"id": "b3921694-0d0f-4030-b0f1-9442db0976f6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Material culture and everyday objects", "children": [{"id": "d7667232-8c37-4660-939a-70ed28c96770", "type": "concept", "title": "Use, wear, and circulation"}, {"id": "8166cdfb-1c7e-4e59-a0ad-de9c84e69a20", "type": "concept", "title": "Craft, industry, and consumption"}, {"id": "409c44c4-7904-449b-9c47-85659b520f54", "type": "concept", "title": "Museums, collecting, and display"}], "description": "Ordinary objects reveal labor, taste, exchange, technology, and social hierarchy."}]}, {"id": "01698aea-510a-42f9-95e9-cd56caebe52c", "type": "topic", "title": "Music, Performance, Film, and Embodied Culture", "description": "The humanities include sound, movement, screen media, and live performance. This topic broadens cultural literacy beyond written and static artifacts.", "children": [{"id": "2c341682-69a5-4399-aaa8-f6f3fbbb1211", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Music as cultural form", "children": [{"id": "31296a15-0b9e-4aec-b361-068b8e5293b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Rhythm, melody, harmony, timbre, and form"}, {"id": "abf1da62-4df2-4c11-b0f4-4f26b7dd93d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Popular, classical, folk, sacred, and global traditions"}, {"id": "fcde9a63-3b7f-4732-ae00-7aa10e7f3e93", "type": "concept", "title": "Listening, notation, and recording"}], "description": "Students learn to hear music as organized sound shaped by history, technology, community, and power."}, {"id": "c55d2355-14bd-4c82-9f62-5db459ae17e7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Performance and embodiment", "children": [{"id": "b19890e4-84a9-48bc-bd2e-e82b5317af55", "type": "concept", "title": "Dance, theatre, ritual, and spoken word"}, {"id": "f044da5a-cf62-492c-aac1-9950fa4d520f", "type": "concept", "title": "Presence, improvisation, and rehearsal"}, {"id": "8c567298-0325-4576-a258-57d37ad602e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender, race, disability, and embodiment"}], "description": "Performance reveals how bodies, gestures, space, and audience co-create meaning."}, {"id": "6659a958-020d-4bfa-b013-712fa66bf801", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Film and screen media", "children": [{"id": "2fbfac21-c080-4384-9ade-0ff4c17d4408", "type": "concept", "title": "Cinematography, editing, sound, and mise-en-scene"}, {"id": "23a77ae2-3a0e-4b18-9fb8-e3fa24a6ca3b", "type": "concept", "title": "Genre and spectatorship"}, {"id": "fbf21c75-1696-4a38-bc49-b6a8192ddf00", "type": "concept", "title": "Documentary, fiction, animation, and streaming culture"}], "description": "Students analyze moving images as narrative, aesthetic, industrial, and ideological systems."}]}, {"id": "7ff9830e-4aa1-49d8-81eb-3307e72e4cb1", "type": "topic", "title": "Language, Translation, Communication, and Media", "description": "Language is never neutral. This topic helps students understand how meaning travels across cultures, media, institutions, and power relations.", "children": [{"id": "318139f7-406e-4be7-8f47-5268e31546df", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Language, meaning, and discourse", "children": [{"id": "3d8cfb32-d303-483c-969c-76fe949c53e0", "type": "concept", "title": "Sign, symbol, metaphor, and narrative"}, {"id": "7fae89b9-6356-4f8d-860a-b2bca4aae40a", "type": "concept", "title": "Discourse communities"}, {"id": "921066e2-867a-4f4a-8be1-73663b7ec8c1", "type": "concept", "title": "Framing and naming"}], "description": "Students study how words structure perception, identity, expertise, and social belonging."}, {"id": "8eb310fe-c104-4484-886b-544b7b83f835", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Translation and cultural mediation", "children": [{"id": "be803daa-f9c8-4585-bf0a-c4ea16663e4d", "type": "concept", "title": "Literal and dynamic equivalence"}, {"id": "e68fda45-4070-49e7-8ebe-3d38a10c20fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Untranslatable concepts"}, {"id": "ed0ec2d2-ff4e-4efe-8f13-160c6ec3e176", "type": "concept", "title": "Translator visibility and cultural context"}], "description": "Translation is studied as interpretation, negotiation, and ethical responsibility."}, {"id": "f385e9ee-0491-42a9-a154-8114d9c26f3f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Media literacy and public communication", "children": [{"id": "e14f72b5-1947-4013-bbdc-577ac6a55a47", "type": "concept", "title": "Authorship and credibility"}, {"id": "4348f3ad-c755-4fd4-8a7b-9bf3df28b28e", "type": "concept", "title": "Algorithms and attention"}, {"id": "7f1f8bc9-7319-423f-8412-09ed4870e806", "type": "concept", "title": "Persuasion, misinformation, and publics"}], "description": "Students learn to evaluate messages across print, broadcast, digital, and social platforms."}]}, {"id": "0885d36d-bc5d-446d-b670-25ede9491f9d", "type": "topic", "title": "Cultural Studies, Identity, Race, Gender, Class, and Power", "description": "Cultural studies asks how everyday life, representation, institutions, and identity are shaped by power. This topic gives students vocabulary for rigorous analysis without reducing culture to slogans.", "children": [{"id": "7d27414c-a3dc-47f8-9a0a-3b77e5085401", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Representation and ideology", "children": [{"id": "2dc2882b-1750-4a53-8463-b8b71b2c796a", "type": "concept", "title": "Stereotype, gaze, and framing"}, {"id": "aa5587a2-5aa7-44c6-8628-3a4870cdb959", "type": "concept", "title": "Hegemony and common sense"}, {"id": "126bdea0-bb74-486d-b057-903ccfba5ff7", "type": "concept", "title": "Counter-narratives and resistance"}], "description": "Students examine how media, literature, images, and institutions normalize certain stories while marginalizing others."}, {"id": "a0c610ca-39e4-400e-96e8-625223c8beec", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Intersectional identity", "children": [{"id": "f3186e93-f3fa-4104-858f-41f43f80da96", "type": "concept", "title": "Intersectionality as method"}, {"id": "97201335-e3c5-469e-aab7-72286da732c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Identity formation and recognition"}, {"id": "1e64309a-b4d9-4dab-9878-9a3b422d3b3f", "type": "concept", "title": "Structural inequality and agency"}], "description": "Students learn how race, gender, sexuality, class, disability, nationality, and religion interact in lived experience."}, {"id": "9168cbaa-9abe-4772-a9f0-b0b04cdd6a69", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Culture, institutions, and everyday life", "children": [{"id": "355ce108-3839-4043-aa17-cd6e1bacc4c8", "type": "concept", "title": "Consumer culture and taste"}, {"id": "ff628ddf-0741-42cc-9015-3f69d9b2099f", "type": "concept", "title": "Subcultures and belonging"}, {"id": "f8c23a6e-c80e-45f2-898e-ed23345625d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Work, home, school, and public space"}], "description": "This subtopic connects large systems to ordinary practices, habits, spaces, and pleasures."}]}, {"id": "45b1a2c8-cf07-476a-ba69-a9c11f6a7388", "type": "topic", "title": "Social Sciences for Humanistic Inquiry: Anthropology, Sociology, Psychology, and Politics", "description": "Distribution learning benefits from social-science methods when they illuminate culture, behavior, institutions, and social patterns.", "children": [{"id": "373a7652-91d8-4966-ba20-28b04392c793", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Anthropological culture and ethnography", "children": [{"id": "e07f8031-63cd-467f-b780-58b1de3f6ce4", "type": "concept", "title": "Participant observation"}, {"id": "e492cbdd-5c07-46dd-b981-9a007b15c731", "type": "concept", "title": "Thick description"}, {"id": "aa59deb7-e871-498a-b2e4-30cc86c507c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Kinship, ritual, exchange, and worldview"}], "description": "Students learn to interpret practices and meanings within cultural contexts while reflecting on their own assumptions."}, {"id": "6013dedc-f685-4ead-911e-c493187e96f2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sociology of institutions and inequality", "children": [{"id": "7529a7ff-bc90-45ab-9b98-20ba74dcffee", "type": "concept", "title": "Class, status, and social mobility"}, {"id": "7528b8ec-8485-4f26-988a-61eb0ada7f66", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutions and organizations"}, {"id": "89ff95a4-41aa-40a6-af82-ea3319152ed6", "type": "concept", "title": "Social norms and deviance"}], "description": "Sociological thinking helps students see how social structures shape choices and opportunities."}, {"id": "df960ddf-f04b-4911-96c8-952bdf8a1a15", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Psychology, politics, and human behavior", "children": [{"id": "1038c9c6-14cc-4a8c-9416-6e9056cfe779", "type": "concept", "title": "Memory, perception, bias, and identity"}, {"id": "df61636a-11c7-4e9e-961e-1a9c17462fd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Group behavior and persuasion"}, {"id": "5d9964ee-bc75-4c5a-8c92-e2f71fc23304", "type": "concept", "title": "Power, legitimacy, and public opinion"}], "description": "Students connect individual cognition and emotion to collective life and governance."}]}, {"id": "998bb36b-3925-459b-a18c-23cf3b192a1d", "type": "topic", "title": "Global and Comparative Humanities: Cross-Cultural Encounters", "description": "A world-class humanities distribution course should resist a single-civilization frame. Students compare traditions, exchanges, conflicts, and translations across regions.", "children": [{"id": "51f57b59-d064-4050-aecc-e5e1bfd3616d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "World literary and artistic traditions", "children": [{"id": "ea78a35f-3307-4dc9-9ddb-393b82ae6935", "type": "concept", "title": "Africa, Asia, the Americas, Europe, Oceania, and the Middle East"}, {"id": "943827d6-3e7d-4a66-b467-78669f097124", "type": "concept", "title": "Oral, manuscript, print, digital, and visual traditions"}, {"id": "980bda82-4e41-4d22-9ced-a8c11538002b", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative genre and theme"}], "description": "Students encounter multiple canons and learn why canon formation itself is a historical process."}, {"id": "150366d3-d943-479e-a9e1-7915d4a9b6fe", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Empire, diaspora, and encounter", "children": [{"id": "f3dbabcf-f2ef-4e1d-97ad-af77bb00cbaa", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonialism and decolonization"}, {"id": "09e3f601-dc6a-465f-b6c8-255e8812d333", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora and hybridity"}, {"id": "20383b66-6dd1-4cfc-92d4-4907c7c9f277", "type": "concept", "title": "Trade routes, missions, and cultural exchange"}], "description": "This subtopic examines how conquest, migration, trade, and translation reshape cultures."}, {"id": "8bead1e2-866e-4d06-8743-b4642b179845", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative ethics and worldviews", "children": [{"id": "16ce8b2c-9e33-41e4-be05-393d78386a7d", "type": "concept", "title": "Human rights and cultural relativism"}, {"id": "91e358b6-163f-4185-9c7a-ad6923547779", "type": "concept", "title": "Community, duty, virtue, and liberation"}, {"id": "7e2ec5cd-ac9f-437c-84d1-70a95c2d0165", "type": "concept", "title": "Conflict, reconciliation, and justice"}], "description": "Students compare moral vocabularies and models of human flourishing across traditions."}]}, {"id": "fbaac852-3c9d-440d-acd1-dd85d17d519a", "type": "topic", "title": "Science, Technology, Environment, and the Humanities", "description": "Humanistic inquiry helps students ask not only what technology can do, but what it means, whom it serves, and how it changes our worlds.", "children": [{"id": "fc3635b2-7e19-4faf-8a74-1ca1caba8afe", "type": "subtopic", "title": "History and philosophy of science", "children": [{"id": "cf2c62ee-2206-4a30-8b93-0d31ae45588c", "type": "concept", "title": "Observation, experiment, model, and theory"}, {"id": "a4ec7606-1e00-4b27-9777-6bfb714dc534", "type": "concept", "title": "Paradigm change and scientific controversy"}, {"id": "19709822-627c-49cc-9d79-099f3d4baff2", "type": "concept", "title": "Expertise, trust, and uncertainty"}], "description": "Students study science as a powerful knowledge system with methods, institutions, values, and histories."}, {"id": "db5a4eba-b794-4218-b52f-3722676c8f6c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Technology, design, and society", "children": [{"id": "bb4e342d-15d7-47cd-86a6-ecc1bb0dd703", "type": "concept", "title": "Technological determinism and social construction"}, {"id": "e19f9e19-6abd-4c84-9bf3-7073eb96b568", "type": "concept", "title": "Design justice and accessibility"}, {"id": "bc47f47a-bf14-4c2d-baaf-509dd1de5fa2", "type": "concept", "title": "Surveillance, platforms, and automation"}], "description": "This subtopic connects artifacts and infrastructures to ethics, labor, access, and imagination."}, {"id": "6b7e7774-aa9a-482d-a4e7-fa54814c793c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Environmental humanities", "children": [{"id": "1fc14a7b-5f07-468e-afd7-8c337d5dc2bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Nature writing and eco-criticism"}, {"id": "77ce57b6-de2f-4d72-bf16-a144f79012a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Indigenous ecological knowledge"}, {"id": "3899529d-2c9a-418d-82b0-ef971aef2da4", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate memory, grief, and action"}], "description": "Students examine land, climate, extraction, species, and place through narrative, ethics, history, and art."}]}, {"id": "278e8e49-9429-4941-93b8-d8a4f95548d6", "type": "topic", "title": "Digital Humanities, Archives, Data, and Public Scholarship", "description": "Digital tools can expand humanistic research, but they also introduce questions about access, interpretation, labor, ownership, and scale.", "children": [{"id": "4abca816-e9d9-43a6-a3a0-bda6cdf28354", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital archives and collections", "children": [{"id": "4346deaf-56d6-4f0d-9232-189a27d749aa", "type": "concept", "title": "Metadata and classification"}, {"id": "edb17be1-fc7b-41dc-9043-403e5e7087c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Search bias and discoverability"}, {"id": "a8465122-7622-4f20-b709-bc3966088802", "type": "concept", "title": "Copyright, access, and preservation"}], "description": "Students learn how digitized collections are built, searched, described, and interpreted."}, {"id": "3cf34dee-be13-4654-bded-b10783bc9e45", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Humanities data and visualization", "children": [{"id": "e48df17b-2a16-4c2d-aa81-8faba65fa5b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Text mining and topic modeling"}, {"id": "1c4a9acb-eaa1-4fd8-9000-0b423e99c4d6", "type": "concept", "title": "GIS and spatial humanities"}, {"id": "c3a1a7e7-35c9-4452-b3f1-60d651f64534", "type": "concept", "title": "Network analysis and visualization ethics"}], "description": "Students experiment with maps, timelines, networks, text analysis, and exhibits while asking what is lost in abstraction."}, {"id": "d195660f-90f8-49a6-9b25-cc4effabe354", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public humanities and audiences", "children": [{"id": "bdc69063-37ec-4740-8829-c8265355883a", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience design"}, {"id": "107ebdb1-cdab-4863-88d6-66f31268bbd4", "type": "concept", "title": "Plain-language interpretation"}, {"id": "7622f3b9-73b7-4c07-9ad7-fca3ca6cd5ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Collaboration and accountability"}], "description": "This subtopic turns scholarship outward through exhibits, essays, podcasts, and community projects."}]}, {"id": "a0dc0c7a-afe3-48b0-9142-825fa84513c0", "type": "topic", "title": "Urban Humanities, Place, Migration, and Everyday Life", "description": "For architecture and design-adjacent students, place is a bridge between humanities, social science, and professional practice.", "children": [{"id": "2b72121a-baf9-443f-98b1-b19354307b07", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reading cities and landscapes", "children": [{"id": "75e587a9-9327-40a2-b30f-a33559b3dae3", "type": "concept", "title": "Spatial justice and segregation"}, {"id": "29420bf5-0671-410d-9e0c-71beee21eb8d", "type": "concept", "title": "Public space and commons"}, {"id": "102ee05e-b25b-45d7-ba6a-79c8c0965d13", "type": "concept", "title": "Landscape, memory, and ecology"}], "description": "Students learn to interpret streets, neighborhoods, monuments, borders, and infrastructures as cultural texts."}, {"id": "41ae50cc-288c-4adf-b35a-c360f662fbfc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Migration, home, and belonging", "children": [{"id": "1686e071-7bc7-4c4f-b7ee-012aa70bc323", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora and exile"}, {"id": "2672c41a-77c4-48c9-8e9e-d508baeb4c20", "type": "concept", "title": "Borders and citizenship"}, {"id": "587f82fd-5ddf-488e-9da6-1bd5a1b24740", "type": "concept", "title": "Home, displacement, and memory"}], "description": "This subtopic examines how movement changes identities, families, cities, languages, and institutions."}, {"id": "492531c3-00e3-4fe9-acc2-697542b443d8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Everyday life and the built environment", "children": [{"id": "21b53e48-a876-415c-8a23-89b1311bfe90", "type": "concept", "title": "Walking, dwelling, work, and leisure"}, {"id": "66d3ff74-e69f-422d-abd7-8b27e8268a0d", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessibility and care"}, {"id": "202c1a9a-0174-4ab4-8399-736f09a3f8b2", "type": "concept", "title": "Informal urbanism and adaptation"}], "description": "Students connect design to ordinary routines, social interaction, and quality of life."}]}, {"id": "5bb38d43-6b19-4a1d-bc42-c51f6b4e8ff3", "type": "topic", "title": "Creative Practice, Interpretation, and Interdisciplinary Making", "description": "Humanities learning deepens when students make as well as analyze. Creative practice helps students understand constraints, audience, form, and revision from the inside.", "children": [{"id": "cf66871f-cafc-4b88-bcea-aa09a41ea057", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Creative response as analysis", "children": [{"id": "ebaee010-5a7d-4597-9913-0a0d19bb05ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Adaptation and remix"}, {"id": "8eaf3a8c-2c17-4034-b5b6-190f85167cfe", "type": "concept", "title": "Ekphrasis and response writing"}, {"id": "516d4788-a099-42ad-afda-c6d88763a69f", "type": "concept", "title": "Artist statements and critical reflection"}], "description": "Students use creative work to test interpretation, not to avoid rigor."}, {"id": "777fa92e-432f-4a5f-93f9-81fd7ff02363", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Studio, workshop, and critique", "children": [{"id": "0510f6eb-2a67-478f-a7a5-e60bf8a8f964", "type": "concept", "title": "Describing before judging"}, {"id": "a2fa164f-e2c1-4bb9-ad1a-e29f5e21b068", "type": "concept", "title": "Intent, effect, and audience"}, {"id": "c001e8af-2db6-4a2c-bc89-b6e2eb2e55f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Revision plans"}], "description": "Critique teaches students how to give evidence-based feedback and receive it productively."}, {"id": "0f69aff4-1235-40c0-8183-927a76b8b300", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interdisciplinary projects", "children": [{"id": "2023c672-ace2-4037-8131-81423863c84e", "type": "concept", "title": "Exhibits and zines"}, {"id": "858faa4c-19f0-4123-bae2-c944d33774fe", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital stories and podcasts"}, {"id": "ced71f4a-dc50-4a92-84cc-2502bdeb978a", "type": "concept", "title": "Place-based design research"}], "description": "Students combine text, image, sound, map, performance, or spatial design into coherent inquiry."}]}, {"id": "80f95e31-9d50-4a7d-9941-3302ab889577", "type": "topic", "title": "Capstone: Integrative Humanities Portfolio, Public Essay, or Presentation", "description": "The capstone asks students to synthesize course learning into a polished piece of interpretation for a real audience.", "children": [{"id": "8af4327d-2c97-4312-ba71-84b673d512b1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research question and source design", "children": [{"id": "c4219d16-8b36-43c9-a44a-d02ee97a19de", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary and secondary source mix"}, {"id": "b0829ace-a043-45fe-8ede-72b4d9244847", "type": "concept", "title": "Annotated bibliography"}, {"id": "4f5b905f-6ab4-4340-a358-e07790ab8ad0", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethical use and citation"}], "description": "Students develop a focused question and select materials that make rigorous interpretation possible."}, {"id": "55ad3d7f-1566-4c17-baa5-5e12bd1c1470", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Integrative argument or creative scholarship", "children": [{"id": "3bba11d0-eab7-41f8-99bd-bdb2aedb98b6", "type": "concept", "title": "Analytical essay"}, {"id": "c4909312-0c87-45c6-998c-96cb83e5c2a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Curated exhibit or visual essay"}, {"id": "98e6449b-d6d4-48a4-9103-ec90d87f99da", "type": "concept", "title": "Podcast, presentation, or design dossier"}], "description": "Students build a project that demonstrates synthesis across disciplines, methods, and media."}, {"id": "36af367c-db4c-45a9-8bd2-8d06f393c098", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reflection and public presentation", "children": [{"id": "60c740ff-0632-42dc-b109-e1066ac36805", "type": "concept", "title": "Process reflection"}, {"id": "dd0cdec4-46e0-481f-8121-a18529dfbeb2", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience-centered presentation"}, {"id": "1c1206eb-3c64-4cdb-afa3-0c321928b4bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Peer review and revision"}], "description": "Students explain what they learned, why it matters, and how their thinking changed."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$lahdjson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'liberal-arts-humanities-distribution'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'Liberal Arts / Humanities Distribution',
  goal = 'Liberal Arts / Humanities Distribution',
  summary = 'This course introduces students to the liberal arts and humanities as disciplined ways of interpreting texts, images, places, performances, beliefs, histories, and cultural practices. It is especially useful for architecture and design-adjacent students because it connects visual/spatial literacy with writing, ethical reasoning, historical context, social analysis, and global cultural awareness.',
  data = $lahdjson${"slug": "liberal-arts-humanities-distribution", "title": "Liberal Arts / Humanities Distribution", "description": "This course introduces students to the liberal arts and humanities as disciplined ways of interpreting texts, images, places, performances, beliefs, histories, and cultural practices. It is especially useful for architecture and design-adjacent students because it connects visual/spatial literacy with writing, ethical reasoning, historical context, social analysis, and global cultural awareness.", "topics": [{"id": "f206b11f-f077-4a22-ae31-7f75735b4681", "type": "topic", "title": "Liberal Arts and Humanities: Purpose, Methods, and Public Value", "description": "This opening topic explains why humanities learning belongs in professional and design education: it builds judgment, interpretation, ethical awareness, cultural literacy, and the ability to connect specialized work to human contexts.", "children": [{"id": "437fddc3-c73e-4ba0-b1ba-b7f3408afeb5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "What the humanities study", "children": [{"id": "54f131d2-83a9-4a04-af4b-97147062f0f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Human meaning, memory, value, and expression"}, {"id": "6478f29e-5b50-4b2c-9546-3dea69e539ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Interpretation rather than only measurement"}, {"id": "87f74d6e-646d-4271-99fe-88bac8e02036", "type": "concept", "title": "Dialogue between past and present"}], "description": "Students learn the range of humanistic inquiry: texts, images, artifacts, places, performances, beliefs, languages, institutions, and lived experience."}, {"id": "f9509b9c-55b2-4bf5-adb3-a8577054d851", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Liberal education and civic judgment", "children": [{"id": "0a79b438-1a8e-4d7b-8445-336e98582bea", "type": "concept", "title": "Civic literacy and democratic conversation"}, {"id": "c0b03585-48a7-4bf5-b9b8-a405e7f80a8e", "type": "concept", "title": "Intellectual humility and curiosity"}, {"id": "272338b9-ede9-4bcf-8d7b-5a70d3797c2a", "type": "concept", "title": "Professional judgment beyond technical competence"}], "description": "This subtopic shows how broad learning develops flexible thinking, public reasoning, and the ability to deliberate across difference."}, {"id": "ee347fe4-8454-44dc-b362-a90ee54baa88", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interdisciplinary habits of mind", "children": [{"id": "905cd122-dc7f-42c9-b104-2dc25fbf7311", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparing disciplinary questions"}, {"id": "2352df86-4a19-46a7-8fee-4413f60034a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Choosing appropriate evidence"}, {"id": "8b13ac87-80be-42d0-b2c3-958ec2145d71", "type": "concept", "title": "Synthesizing rather than merely sampling"}], "description": "Students practice moving between literature, history, philosophy, art, religion, and social science without flattening their distinct methods."}]}, {"id": "494df0c9-0d8a-4753-becb-d26548aa6cd7", "type": "topic", "title": "Critical Reading, Close Looking, and Interpretive Evidence", "description": "Humanities courses train attention. This topic builds the core skill of slowing down, observing carefully, and turning evidence into persuasive interpretation.", "children": [{"id": "cfd59fc1-eb87-47d3-ab13-9ec93d11b539", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Close reading of texts", "children": [{"id": "fbdb420f-59a9-4a62-9bcc-4d7267653556", "type": "concept", "title": "Diction, imagery, tone, and syntax"}, {"id": "d09f17d2-dc9e-4993-95e5-c37827226c93", "type": "concept", "title": "Narrator, audience, and point of view"}, {"id": "5efb3223-6f48-4519-9341-194959e1b47c", "type": "concept", "title": "Patterns, tensions, and ambiguities"}], "description": "Students learn how wording, structure, voice, genre, and omission shape meaning."}, {"id": "25bc4299-614c-477f-b5a7-beb209ec67ee", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Close looking at images and objects", "children": [{"id": "653ab2d1-a943-4509-8b80-271059712c63", "type": "concept", "title": "Composition, scale, color, material, and technique"}, {"id": "0ad24ce5-6a4d-4d46-86ee-07b0b50ddb46", "type": "concept", "title": "Iconography and symbolism"}, {"id": "69798004-c97c-402d-b6aa-278d35a583cb", "type": "concept", "title": "Site, viewer, and use"}], "description": "Visual analysis helps students describe what they see before jumping to interpretation or judgment."}, {"id": "967a0b6b-cf20-4c76-bd88-9c80723f62de", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evidence, inference, and interpretation", "children": [{"id": "99ceb4fe-8a23-4b75-abec-98b542af8933", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary and secondary evidence"}, {"id": "006c6bf6-2922-4a46-8f8f-9eaaa42c3699", "type": "concept", "title": "Contextual inference"}, {"id": "0edaf6ab-9be8-42a4-9464-6cb09de94076", "type": "concept", "title": "Competing interpretations"}], "description": "Students distinguish evidence from opinion and learn how claims become credible in humanistic work."}]}, {"id": "a7b1eb9e-410e-484f-b035-0e5f43c8f816", "type": "topic", "title": "Writing, Argument, Rhetoric, and Revision", "description": "A distribution course should leave students better able to write clearly, reason publicly, and revise their own thinking. This topic makes writing a central intellectual practice.", "children": [{"id": "615dd5b0-52e7-4107-9e3d-a5df5509c4e9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Claims, stakes, and thesis development", "children": [{"id": "0b1eb6c4-1cc2-4a11-8880-b2831a88890a", "type": "concept", "title": "Analytical questions"}, {"id": "0ec2d4a6-b756-402c-8af3-60101c382d99", "type": "concept", "title": "Thesis scope and contestability"}, {"id": "d25c2fe4-5bf1-45cf-8093-c3d158d87fdd", "type": "concept", "title": "Stakes for readers and fields"}], "description": "Students learn to make arguments that matter, not just summaries that report information."}, {"id": "f0a90795-9bb7-40e8-b3a2-27f2ddaf774e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Rhetorical awareness", "children": [{"id": "63467ad2-66eb-4281-bf98-34fccb1b11b1", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethos, pathos, logos, and kairos"}, {"id": "94344d3e-4721-4628-b41e-4e753ddeacde", "type": "concept", "title": "Style, clarity, and voice"}, {"id": "de7fcf33-4bbe-436b-81ad-22628bd46357", "type": "concept", "title": "Public, academic, and professional genres"}], "description": "Students examine how audience, purpose, genre, and medium shape communication."}, {"id": "8f3a1163-351d-477e-bb85-70388c3c5872", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Revision as thinking", "children": [{"id": "e566a064-6306-4d72-99d1-6db71aca4f70", "type": "concept", "title": "Reverse outlining"}, {"id": "4a381ff3-1b65-4336-9e06-0d3e93c17710", "type": "concept", "title": "Peer feedback and response"}, {"id": "53ff7221-653a-429a-86a6-5029eb24eaa0", "type": "concept", "title": "Editing for precision and rhythm"}], "description": "Revision is treated as a method for sharpening evidence, structure, and intellectual honesty."}]}, {"id": "c9447b9a-0af0-40ec-b015-020e14407fef", "type": "topic", "title": "Literature, Narrative, Poetry, and Drama", "description": "Literary study expands attention to language, imagination, identity, genre, and the emotional complexity of human experience.", "children": [{"id": "bf4675b2-d8cb-49b6-9939-4431a315bed5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Narrative and fiction", "children": [{"id": "512b38ed-58fa-4995-8a00-7f6679a3c038", "type": "concept", "title": "Plot, setting, and characterization"}, {"id": "65deede9-afe6-4f5a-b05e-0f0de457e48d", "type": "concept", "title": "Unreliable narration and perspective"}, {"id": "34cabbea-163b-461b-ad09-c58a5ecd0038", "type": "concept", "title": "Realism, modernism, speculative fiction, and world literature"}], "description": "Students study how stories organize time, character, causality, memory, and social worlds."}, {"id": "047bfc65-010c-4496-a765-60394f6680d7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Poetry and compressed language", "children": [{"id": "5dff3aec-7c59-44ec-a4c7-9edc468f6419", "type": "concept", "title": "Line, meter, rhythm, and form"}, {"id": "046f8123-b9f8-4e7f-ad73-db3e91f9c51f", "type": "concept", "title": "Image, metaphor, and symbol"}, {"id": "60ff64e4-da8b-4408-b3dc-8fc447815024", "type": "concept", "title": "Voice, lyric, and performance"}], "description": "Poetry teaches precision, pattern, sound, and ambiguity in their most concentrated forms."}, {"id": "95bb5b04-0d64-46e7-861b-5f174256fe13", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Drama and performance", "children": [{"id": "a46898fc-0654-4abb-bda7-90aa81e1bdde", "type": "concept", "title": "Dialogue, staging, and conflict"}, {"id": "b271a6b6-41be-4fd5-ad99-83320293880a", "type": "concept", "title": "Tragedy, comedy, ritual, and modern theatre"}, {"id": "83a2ec49-482a-4b71-ad6a-39ff8fba21f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience, performance, and adaptation"}], "description": "Drama reveals how text becomes embodied action in shared space."}]}, {"id": "f6e6c853-b16e-4cb3-a5e8-63c1f25af431", "type": "topic", "title": "History, Memory, Archives, and Historical Thinking", "description": "Historical thinking helps students understand change over time, evaluate sources, and see how societies remember, forget, and argue about the past.", "children": [{"id": "c151a968-e3da-4b32-b765-250b9eed4e5f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical questions and causation", "children": [{"id": "9f8dfb64-4e03-49e2-ba6e-cf00d41f1812", "type": "concept", "title": "Chronology and periodization"}, {"id": "e8da36f3-117c-46f8-b815-250a71edf4a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Causation and contingency"}, {"id": "76bea439-9def-4a0e-af23-e20c61f6b2c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Scale: local, national, regional, and global"}], "description": "Students learn to ask questions about change, continuity, contingency, and consequence."}, {"id": "8e44bfe4-c9da-4347-987b-d18f4fcba8dd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Primary sources and archives", "children": [{"id": "67db98a9-030a-47c0-beb0-9e8c9f63ccf1", "type": "concept", "title": "Manuscripts, newspapers, photographs, maps, oral histories"}, {"id": "3273673b-1fb4-49c4-b95a-07c438291a06", "type": "concept", "title": "Archival silences and power"}, {"id": "ce616bff-e983-422a-a25c-9ccdb9b343b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Citation and provenance"}], "description": "Students learn how historical evidence is created, preserved, classified, and interpreted."}, {"id": "d18a86f3-23b8-4b20-b193-2604eb57a19b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Memory, monuments, and public history", "children": [{"id": "0aef0a2a-cc4e-4825-9edd-23f32930ec8c", "type": "concept", "title": "Museums and memorials"}, {"id": "180d96a8-2a9c-41dc-8ff0-ea415670fa25", "type": "concept", "title": "Contested monuments"}, {"id": "6caed7ef-2e0c-43d3-86f2-0bcbc9cc1025", "type": "concept", "title": "Community history and public scholarship"}], "description": "This subtopic connects scholarship to public debates about heritage, commemoration, and collective identity."}]}, {"id": "de518dbb-5fd8-4f3c-9a83-7bcab97b9f6a", "type": "topic", "title": "Philosophy, Ethics, Meaning, and Critical Reasoning", "description": "Philosophy gives students tools for clarifying concepts, testing assumptions, and asking what follows if their beliefs are true.", "children": [{"id": "5fe51cc3-30cb-48c8-964d-210dd8293287", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Logic and argument analysis", "children": [{"id": "8cd9fb97-b482-4493-9366-17deb17acac4", "type": "concept", "title": "Deductive and inductive arguments"}, {"id": "aab3ce39-82aa-4bc1-a836-61dae0bf6d8a", "type": "concept", "title": "Fallacies and charitable reconstruction"}, {"id": "f865fbe6-22ff-496a-a639-8136a1266d5b", "type": "concept", "title": "Definitions and conceptual clarity"}], "description": "Students learn to identify valid reasoning, hidden assumptions, and weak inferences."}, {"id": "ec0c8bdc-4798-4712-a3d8-38a8800765b2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethical theories and moral judgment", "children": [{"id": "b370640d-9217-4c2a-b0ce-64a26cd9f899", "type": "concept", "title": "Consequentialism, deontology, virtue ethics, care ethics"}, {"id": "529e3892-565c-42ae-8ce5-a2d6bc580b60", "type": "concept", "title": "Rights, duties, harms, and goods"}, {"id": "e51925c5-7657-4473-b619-6622a6b0999b", "type": "concept", "title": "Professional and civic ethics"}], "description": "Students compare major approaches to moral reasoning and apply them to difficult cases."}, {"id": "7b825a0a-8077-4c0a-836e-397ba6fedee5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Meaning, knowledge, and reality", "children": [{"id": "a20b5351-e333-4443-85db-af26cd9dd8a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Epistemology and skepticism"}, {"id": "7757712d-8064-4dbb-b149-acde0e20f94e", "type": "concept", "title": "Personhood and identity"}, {"id": "4368dedc-1503-48ac-9e97-e4c8111c0fe8", "type": "concept", "title": "Free will, responsibility, and meaning"}], "description": "Classic questions about truth, knowledge, freedom, identity, and value deepen students' ability to think at the root level."}]}, {"id": "23c52b3e-5966-4847-9ac7-0785dffb5d37", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion, Myth, Ritual, and Symbolic Worlds", "description": "This topic treats religion as a major human form of meaning-making, social organization, artistic expression, ethical imagination, and conflict.", "children": [{"id": "a3e30b57-fe61-43e0-85a9-fbff050a8d1a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scripture, story, and interpretation", "children": [{"id": "6b430c29-63c0-44fb-87e5-15bbc95cc4c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon, commentary, and reception"}, {"id": "e26612c0-4041-4924-97a2-cc6bf6ab9ece", "type": "concept", "title": "Creation, exile, liberation, wisdom, and apocalypse"}, {"id": "4f428c84-403f-4fda-8ed9-7d29a84aa017", "type": "concept", "title": "Hermeneutics and translation"}], "description": "Students examine sacred texts and myths as layered works shaped by translation, tradition, and community."}, {"id": "a7519637-ae50-4178-8b62-b6bf0ce029eb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ritual, practice, and community", "children": [{"id": "a7b0cdd0-b732-4023-911a-2dd373db2060", "type": "concept", "title": "Pilgrimage, prayer, festival, and sacrifice"}, {"id": "98902f86-85e1-4dfe-ba05-5eb5c5afc003", "type": "concept", "title": "Embodiment and material religion"}, {"id": "e77d0191-b3d6-4cbb-9a2d-61d9e55a69e6", "type": "concept", "title": "Authority, institutions, and belonging"}], "description": "Religions are studied as lived systems of practice, not only as sets of doctrines."}, {"id": "3b6eb3b5-fefa-47ba-b618-10023973410e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religion, pluralism, and modernity", "children": [{"id": "ba964c71-bb85-4cd5-b48c-994f43c262c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Religion and politics"}, {"id": "f03347b0-2b90-48c2-9101-42eed1b4edbc", "type": "concept", "title": "Pluralism and interfaith ethics"}, {"id": "105cb670-3aaf-43c6-a48c-f9d481e7043c", "type": "concept", "title": "Modern critique and renewal"}], "description": "Students explore secularization, interreligious encounter, fundamentalism, and public life."}]}, {"id": "c9ae0110-842b-4601-a9d8-42835cbdfd07", "type": "topic", "title": "Visual Arts, Architecture, Design, and Material Culture", "description": "Because this course is referenced in architecture, visual and spatial literacy are essential. Students learn how objects, buildings, and images carry cultural arguments.", "children": [{"id": "84b95a28-d56c-4725-be46-d26cde5f3a91", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Art-historical description and context", "children": [{"id": "10d8e07e-5617-4d40-8099-56be602dc9eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Medium, technique, and style"}, {"id": "83ec54b2-796c-4b7e-b1df-56bb63189624", "type": "concept", "title": "Patronage, institution, and audience"}, {"id": "f8d6dab3-bc68-49f4-bf2d-9a37ae980f28", "type": "concept", "title": "Period, region, and movement"}], "description": "Students learn to connect formal description with historical setting and social use."}, {"id": "a40b5864-bdda-4825-859c-32fbb4a39588", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Architecture as cultural text", "children": [{"id": "6a7759b5-445f-4944-8594-4be7d4d9a675", "type": "concept", "title": "Form, function, ornament, and structure"}, {"id": "f6562bee-db7e-4207-b77d-66d157befbda", "type": "concept", "title": "Urban context and public life"}, {"id": "cb27dc4c-537b-4393-a431-a301ef00d2da", "type": "concept", "title": "Power, memory, and identity in built form"}], "description": "Buildings are read as spatial, technical, social, and symbolic artifacts."}, {"id": "b3921694-0d0f-4030-b0f1-9442db0976f6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Material culture and everyday objects", "children": [{"id": "d7667232-8c37-4660-939a-70ed28c96770", "type": "concept", "title": "Use, wear, and circulation"}, {"id": "8166cdfb-1c7e-4e59-a0ad-de9c84e69a20", "type": "concept", "title": "Craft, industry, and consumption"}, {"id": "409c44c4-7904-449b-9c47-85659b520f54", "type": "concept", "title": "Museums, collecting, and display"}], "description": "Ordinary objects reveal labor, taste, exchange, technology, and social hierarchy."}]}, {"id": "01698aea-510a-42f9-95e9-cd56caebe52c", "type": "topic", "title": "Music, Performance, Film, and Embodied Culture", "description": "The humanities include sound, movement, screen media, and live performance. This topic broadens cultural literacy beyond written and static artifacts.", "children": [{"id": "2c341682-69a5-4399-aaa8-f6f3fbbb1211", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Music as cultural form", "children": [{"id": "31296a15-0b9e-4aec-b361-068b8e5293b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Rhythm, melody, harmony, timbre, and form"}, {"id": "abf1da62-4df2-4c11-b0f4-4f26b7dd93d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Popular, classical, folk, sacred, and global traditions"}, {"id": "fcde9a63-3b7f-4732-ae00-7aa10e7f3e93", "type": "concept", "title": "Listening, notation, and recording"}], "description": "Students learn to hear music as organized sound shaped by history, technology, community, and power."}, {"id": "c55d2355-14bd-4c82-9f62-5db459ae17e7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Performance and embodiment", "children": [{"id": "b19890e4-84a9-48bc-bd2e-e82b5317af55", "type": "concept", "title": "Dance, theatre, ritual, and spoken word"}, {"id": "f044da5a-cf62-492c-aac1-9950fa4d520f", "type": "concept", "title": "Presence, improvisation, and rehearsal"}, {"id": "8c567298-0325-4576-a258-57d37ad602e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender, race, disability, and embodiment"}], "description": "Performance reveals how bodies, gestures, space, and audience co-create meaning."}, {"id": "6659a958-020d-4bfa-b013-712fa66bf801", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Film and screen media", "children": [{"id": "2fbfac21-c080-4384-9ade-0ff4c17d4408", "type": "concept", "title": "Cinematography, editing, sound, and mise-en-scene"}, {"id": "23a77ae2-3a0e-4b18-9fb8-e3fa24a6ca3b", "type": "concept", "title": "Genre and spectatorship"}, {"id": "fbf21c75-1696-4a38-bc49-b6a8192ddf00", "type": "concept", "title": "Documentary, fiction, animation, and streaming culture"}], "description": "Students analyze moving images as narrative, aesthetic, industrial, and ideological systems."}]}, {"id": "7ff9830e-4aa1-49d8-81eb-3307e72e4cb1", "type": "topic", "title": "Language, Translation, Communication, and Media", "description": "Language is never neutral. This topic helps students understand how meaning travels across cultures, media, institutions, and power relations.", "children": [{"id": "318139f7-406e-4be7-8f47-5268e31546df", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Language, meaning, and discourse", "children": [{"id": "3d8cfb32-d303-483c-969c-76fe949c53e0", "type": "concept", "title": "Sign, symbol, metaphor, and narrative"}, {"id": "7fae89b9-6356-4f8d-860a-b2bca4aae40a", "type": "concept", "title": "Discourse communities"}, {"id": "921066e2-867a-4f4a-8be1-73663b7ec8c1", "type": "concept", "title": "Framing and naming"}], "description": "Students study how words structure perception, identity, expertise, and social belonging."}, {"id": "8eb310fe-c104-4484-886b-544b7b83f835", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Translation and cultural mediation", "children": [{"id": "be803daa-f9c8-4585-bf0a-c4ea16663e4d", "type": "concept", "title": "Literal and dynamic equivalence"}, {"id": "e68fda45-4070-49e7-8ebe-3d38a10c20fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Untranslatable concepts"}, {"id": "ed0ec2d2-ff4e-4efe-8f13-160c6ec3e176", "type": "concept", "title": "Translator visibility and cultural context"}], "description": "Translation is studied as interpretation, negotiation, and ethical responsibility."}, {"id": "f385e9ee-0491-42a9-a154-8114d9c26f3f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Media literacy and public communication", "children": [{"id": "e14f72b5-1947-4013-bbdc-577ac6a55a47", "type": "concept", "title": "Authorship and credibility"}, {"id": "4348f3ad-c755-4fd4-8a7b-9bf3df28b28e", "type": "concept", "title": "Algorithms and attention"}, {"id": "7f1f8bc9-7319-423f-8412-09ed4870e806", "type": "concept", "title": "Persuasion, misinformation, and publics"}], "description": "Students learn to evaluate messages across print, broadcast, digital, and social platforms."}]}, {"id": "0885d36d-bc5d-446d-b670-25ede9491f9d", "type": "topic", "title": "Cultural Studies, Identity, Race, Gender, Class, and Power", "description": "Cultural studies asks how everyday life, representation, institutions, and identity are shaped by power. This topic gives students vocabulary for rigorous analysis without reducing culture to slogans.", "children": [{"id": "7d27414c-a3dc-47f8-9a0a-3b77e5085401", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Representation and ideology", "children": [{"id": "2dc2882b-1750-4a53-8463-b8b71b2c796a", "type": "concept", "title": "Stereotype, gaze, and framing"}, {"id": "aa5587a2-5aa7-44c6-8628-3a4870cdb959", "type": "concept", "title": "Hegemony and common sense"}, {"id": "126bdea0-bb74-486d-b057-903ccfba5ff7", "type": "concept", "title": "Counter-narratives and resistance"}], "description": "Students examine how media, literature, images, and institutions normalize certain stories while marginalizing others."}, {"id": "a0c610ca-39e4-400e-96e8-625223c8beec", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Intersectional identity", "children": [{"id": "f3186e93-f3fa-4104-858f-41f43f80da96", "type": "concept", "title": "Intersectionality as method"}, {"id": "97201335-e3c5-469e-aab7-72286da732c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Identity formation and recognition"}, {"id": "1e64309a-b4d9-4dab-9878-9a3b422d3b3f", "type": "concept", "title": "Structural inequality and agency"}], "description": "Students learn how race, gender, sexuality, class, disability, nationality, and religion interact in lived experience."}, {"id": "9168cbaa-9abe-4772-a9f0-b0b04cdd6a69", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Culture, institutions, and everyday life", "children": [{"id": "355ce108-3839-4043-aa17-cd6e1bacc4c8", "type": "concept", "title": "Consumer culture and taste"}, {"id": "ff628ddf-0741-42cc-9015-3f69d9b2099f", "type": "concept", "title": "Subcultures and belonging"}, {"id": "f8c23a6e-c80e-45f2-898e-ed23345625d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Work, home, school, and public space"}], "description": "This subtopic connects large systems to ordinary practices, habits, spaces, and pleasures."}]}, {"id": "45b1a2c8-cf07-476a-ba69-a9c11f6a7388", "type": "topic", "title": "Social Sciences for Humanistic Inquiry: Anthropology, Sociology, Psychology, and Politics", "description": "Distribution learning benefits from social-science methods when they illuminate culture, behavior, institutions, and social patterns.", "children": [{"id": "373a7652-91d8-4966-ba20-28b04392c793", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Anthropological culture and ethnography", "children": [{"id": "e07f8031-63cd-467f-b780-58b1de3f6ce4", "type": "concept", "title": "Participant observation"}, {"id": "e492cbdd-5c07-46dd-b981-9a007b15c731", "type": "concept", "title": "Thick description"}, {"id": "aa59deb7-e871-498a-b2e4-30cc86c507c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Kinship, ritual, exchange, and worldview"}], "description": "Students learn to interpret practices and meanings within cultural contexts while reflecting on their own assumptions."}, {"id": "6013dedc-f685-4ead-911e-c493187e96f2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sociology of institutions and inequality", "children": [{"id": "7529a7ff-bc90-45ab-9b98-20ba74dcffee", "type": "concept", "title": "Class, status, and social mobility"}, {"id": "7528b8ec-8485-4f26-988a-61eb0ada7f66", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutions and organizations"}, {"id": "89ff95a4-41aa-40a6-af82-ea3319152ed6", "type": "concept", "title": "Social norms and deviance"}], "description": "Sociological thinking helps students see how social structures shape choices and opportunities."}, {"id": "df960ddf-f04b-4911-96c8-952bdf8a1a15", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Psychology, politics, and human behavior", "children": [{"id": "1038c9c6-14cc-4a8c-9416-6e9056cfe779", "type": "concept", "title": "Memory, perception, bias, and identity"}, {"id": "df61636a-11c7-4e9e-961e-1a9c17462fd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Group behavior and persuasion"}, {"id": "5d9964ee-bc75-4c5a-8c92-e2f71fc23304", "type": "concept", "title": "Power, legitimacy, and public opinion"}], "description": "Students connect individual cognition and emotion to collective life and governance."}]}, {"id": "998bb36b-3925-459b-a18c-23cf3b192a1d", "type": "topic", "title": "Global and Comparative Humanities: Cross-Cultural Encounters", "description": "A world-class humanities distribution course should resist a single-civilization frame. Students compare traditions, exchanges, conflicts, and translations across regions.", "children": [{"id": "51f57b59-d064-4050-aecc-e5e1bfd3616d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "World literary and artistic traditions", "children": [{"id": "ea78a35f-3307-4dc9-9ddb-393b82ae6935", "type": "concept", "title": "Africa, Asia, the Americas, Europe, Oceania, and the Middle East"}, {"id": "943827d6-3e7d-4a66-b467-78669f097124", "type": "concept", "title": "Oral, manuscript, print, digital, and visual traditions"}, {"id": "980bda82-4e41-4d22-9ced-a8c11538002b", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative genre and theme"}], "description": "Students encounter multiple canons and learn why canon formation itself is a historical process."}, {"id": "150366d3-d943-479e-a9e1-7915d4a9b6fe", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Empire, diaspora, and encounter", "children": [{"id": "f3dbabcf-f2ef-4e1d-97ad-af77bb00cbaa", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonialism and decolonization"}, {"id": "09e3f601-dc6a-465f-b6c8-255e8812d333", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora and hybridity"}, {"id": "20383b66-6dd1-4cfc-92d4-4907c7c9f277", "type": "concept", "title": "Trade routes, missions, and cultural exchange"}], "description": "This subtopic examines how conquest, migration, trade, and translation reshape cultures."}, {"id": "8bead1e2-866e-4d06-8743-b4642b179845", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative ethics and worldviews", "children": [{"id": "16ce8b2c-9e33-41e4-be05-393d78386a7d", "type": "concept", "title": "Human rights and cultural relativism"}, {"id": "91e358b6-163f-4185-9c7a-ad6923547779", "type": "concept", "title": "Community, duty, virtue, and liberation"}, {"id": "7e2ec5cd-ac9f-437c-84d1-70a95c2d0165", "type": "concept", "title": "Conflict, reconciliation, and justice"}], "description": "Students compare moral vocabularies and models of human flourishing across traditions."}]}, {"id": "fbaac852-3c9d-440d-acd1-dd85d17d519a", "type": "topic", "title": "Science, Technology, Environment, and the Humanities", "description": "Humanistic inquiry helps students ask not only what technology can do, but what it means, whom it serves, and how it changes our worlds.", "children": [{"id": "fc3635b2-7e19-4faf-8a74-1ca1caba8afe", "type": "subtopic", "title": "History and philosophy of science", "children": [{"id": "cf2c62ee-2206-4a30-8b93-0d31ae45588c", "type": "concept", "title": "Observation, experiment, model, and theory"}, {"id": "a4ec7606-1e00-4b27-9777-6bfb714dc534", "type": "concept", "title": "Paradigm change and scientific controversy"}, {"id": "19709822-627c-49cc-9d79-099f3d4baff2", "type": "concept", "title": "Expertise, trust, and uncertainty"}], "description": "Students study science as a powerful knowledge system with methods, institutions, values, and histories."}, {"id": "db5a4eba-b794-4218-b52f-3722676c8f6c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Technology, design, and society", "children": [{"id": "bb4e342d-15d7-47cd-86a6-ecc1bb0dd703", "type": "concept", "title": "Technological determinism and social construction"}, {"id": "e19f9e19-6abd-4c84-9bf3-7073eb96b568", "type": "concept", "title": "Design justice and accessibility"}, {"id": "bc47f47a-bf14-4c2d-baaf-509dd1de5fa2", "type": "concept", "title": "Surveillance, platforms, and automation"}], "description": "This subtopic connects artifacts and infrastructures to ethics, labor, access, and imagination."}, {"id": "6b7e7774-aa9a-482d-a4e7-fa54814c793c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Environmental humanities", "children": [{"id": "1fc14a7b-5f07-468e-afd7-8c337d5dc2bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Nature writing and eco-criticism"}, {"id": "77ce57b6-de2f-4d72-bf16-a144f79012a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Indigenous ecological knowledge"}, {"id": "3899529d-2c9a-418d-82b0-ef971aef2da4", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate memory, grief, and action"}], "description": "Students examine land, climate, extraction, species, and place through narrative, ethics, history, and art."}]}, {"id": "278e8e49-9429-4941-93b8-d8a4f95548d6", "type": "topic", "title": "Digital Humanities, Archives, Data, and Public Scholarship", "description": "Digital tools can expand humanistic research, but they also introduce questions about access, interpretation, labor, ownership, and scale.", "children": [{"id": "4abca816-e9d9-43a6-a3a0-bda6cdf28354", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital archives and collections", "children": [{"id": "4346deaf-56d6-4f0d-9232-189a27d749aa", "type": "concept", "title": "Metadata and classification"}, {"id": "edb17be1-fc7b-41dc-9043-403e5e7087c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Search bias and discoverability"}, {"id": "a8465122-7622-4f20-b709-bc3966088802", "type": "concept", "title": "Copyright, access, and preservation"}], "description": "Students learn how digitized collections are built, searched, described, and interpreted."}, {"id": "3cf34dee-be13-4654-bded-b10783bc9e45", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Humanities data and visualization", "children": [{"id": "e48df17b-2a16-4c2d-aa81-8faba65fa5b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Text mining and topic modeling"}, {"id": "1c4a9acb-eaa1-4fd8-9000-0b423e99c4d6", "type": "concept", "title": "GIS and spatial humanities"}, {"id": "c3a1a7e7-35c9-4452-b3f1-60d651f64534", "type": "concept", "title": "Network analysis and visualization ethics"}], "description": "Students experiment with maps, timelines, networks, text analysis, and exhibits while asking what is lost in abstraction."}, {"id": "d195660f-90f8-49a6-9b25-cc4effabe354", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public humanities and audiences", "children": [{"id": "bdc69063-37ec-4740-8829-c8265355883a", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience design"}, {"id": "107ebdb1-cdab-4863-88d6-66f31268bbd4", "type": "concept", "title": "Plain-language interpretation"}, {"id": "7622f3b9-73b7-4c07-9ad7-fca3ca6cd5ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Collaboration and accountability"}], "description": "This subtopic turns scholarship outward through exhibits, essays, podcasts, and community projects."}]}, {"id": "a0dc0c7a-afe3-48b0-9142-825fa84513c0", "type": "topic", "title": "Urban Humanities, Place, Migration, and Everyday Life", "description": "For architecture and design-adjacent students, place is a bridge between humanities, social science, and professional practice.", "children": [{"id": "2b72121a-baf9-443f-98b1-b19354307b07", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reading cities and landscapes", "children": [{"id": "75e587a9-9327-40a2-b30f-a33559b3dae3", "type": "concept", "title": "Spatial justice and segregation"}, {"id": "29420bf5-0671-410d-9e0c-71beee21eb8d", "type": "concept", "title": "Public space and commons"}, {"id": "102ee05e-b25b-45d7-ba6a-79c8c0965d13", "type": "concept", "title": "Landscape, memory, and ecology"}], "description": "Students learn to interpret streets, neighborhoods, monuments, borders, and infrastructures as cultural texts."}, {"id": "41ae50cc-288c-4adf-b35a-c360f662fbfc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Migration, home, and belonging", "children": [{"id": "1686e071-7bc7-4c4f-b7ee-012aa70bc323", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora and exile"}, {"id": "2672c41a-77c4-48c9-8e9e-d508baeb4c20", "type": "concept", "title": "Borders and citizenship"}, {"id": "587f82fd-5ddf-488e-9da6-1bd5a1b24740", "type": "concept", "title": "Home, displacement, and memory"}], "description": "This subtopic examines how movement changes identities, families, cities, languages, and institutions."}, {"id": "492531c3-00e3-4fe9-acc2-697542b443d8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Everyday life and the built environment", "children": [{"id": "21b53e48-a876-415c-8a23-89b1311bfe90", "type": "concept", "title": "Walking, dwelling, work, and leisure"}, {"id": "66d3ff74-e69f-422d-abd7-8b27e8268a0d", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessibility and care"}, {"id": "202c1a9a-0174-4ab4-8399-736f09a3f8b2", "type": "concept", "title": "Informal urbanism and adaptation"}], "description": "Students connect design to ordinary routines, social interaction, and quality of life."}]}, {"id": "5bb38d43-6b19-4a1d-bc42-c51f6b4e8ff3", "type": "topic", "title": "Creative Practice, Interpretation, and Interdisciplinary Making", "description": "Humanities learning deepens when students make as well as analyze. Creative practice helps students understand constraints, audience, form, and revision from the inside.", "children": [{"id": "cf66871f-cafc-4b88-bcea-aa09a41ea057", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Creative response as analysis", "children": [{"id": "ebaee010-5a7d-4597-9913-0a0d19bb05ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Adaptation and remix"}, {"id": "8eaf3a8c-2c17-4034-b5b6-190f85167cfe", "type": "concept", "title": "Ekphrasis and response writing"}, {"id": "516d4788-a099-42ad-afda-c6d88763a69f", "type": "concept", "title": "Artist statements and critical reflection"}], "description": "Students use creative work to test interpretation, not to avoid rigor."}, {"id": "777fa92e-432f-4a5f-93f9-81fd7ff02363", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Studio, workshop, and critique", "children": [{"id": "0510f6eb-2a67-478f-a7a5-e60bf8a8f964", "type": "concept", "title": "Describing before judging"}, {"id": "a2fa164f-e2c1-4bb9-ad1a-e29f5e21b068", "type": "concept", "title": "Intent, effect, and audience"}, {"id": "c001e8af-2db6-4a2c-bc89-b6e2eb2e55f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Revision plans"}], "description": "Critique teaches students how to give evidence-based feedback and receive it productively."}, {"id": "0f69aff4-1235-40c0-8183-927a76b8b300", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interdisciplinary projects", "children": [{"id": "2023c672-ace2-4037-8131-81423863c84e", "type": "concept", "title": "Exhibits and zines"}, {"id": "858faa4c-19f0-4123-bae2-c944d33774fe", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital stories and podcasts"}, {"id": "ced71f4a-dc50-4a92-84cc-2502bdeb978a", "type": "concept", "title": "Place-based design research"}], "description": "Students combine text, image, sound, map, performance, or spatial design into coherent inquiry."}]}, {"id": "80f95e31-9d50-4a7d-9941-3302ab889577", "type": "topic", "title": "Capstone: Integrative Humanities Portfolio, Public Essay, or Presentation", "description": "The capstone asks students to synthesize course learning into a polished piece of interpretation for a real audience.", "children": [{"id": "8af4327d-2c97-4312-ba71-84b673d512b1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research question and source design", "children": [{"id": "c4219d16-8b36-43c9-a44a-d02ee97a19de", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary and secondary source mix"}, {"id": "b0829ace-a043-45fe-8ede-72b4d9244847", "type": "concept", "title": "Annotated bibliography"}, {"id": "4f5b905f-6ab4-4340-a358-e07790ab8ad0", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethical use and citation"}], "description": "Students develop a focused question and select materials that make rigorous interpretation possible."}, {"id": "55ad3d7f-1566-4c17-baa5-5e12bd1c1470", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Integrative argument or creative scholarship", "children": [{"id": "3bba11d0-eab7-41f8-99bd-bdb2aedb98b6", "type": "concept", "title": "Analytical essay"}, {"id": "c4909312-0c87-45c6-998c-96cb83e5c2a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Curated exhibit or visual essay"}, {"id": "98e6449b-d6d4-48a4-9103-ec90d87f99da", "type": "concept", "title": "Podcast, presentation, or design dossier"}], "description": "Students build a project that demonstrates synthesis across disciplines, methods, and media."}, {"id": "36af367c-db4c-45a9-8bd2-8d06f393c098", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reflection and public presentation", "children": [{"id": "60c740ff-0632-42dc-b109-e1066ac36805", "type": "concept", "title": "Process reflection"}, {"id": "dd0cdec4-46e0-481f-8121-a18529dfbeb2", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience-centered presentation"}, {"id": "1c1206eb-3c64-4cdb-afa3-0c321928b4bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Peer review and revision"}], "description": "Students explain what they learned, why it matters, and how their thinking changed."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$lahdjson$::jsonb,
